Roger, Walt. Start acceleration checkpoint. 600 miles out at 0722. Let's get it in high gear.

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:01.830
I'm going to do a check point.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:17:59.880
twice the speed of sound.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:02.880
for its final 600-mile dash to the target,

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:07.090
creating, and dragging with it, a thunderclap of sound.

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:11.830
Like all sound, this sonic boom results from an invisible pressure wave.

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:14.510
Below Mach 1, the speed of sound,

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.220
they push aside the molecules just ahead,

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:22.300
and they, in turn, give warning and push aside the molecules ahead of them.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:25.090
But at the exact speed of sound,

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:27.990
these molecules can no longer react quickly enough.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:29.960
The leading edges are among them...

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:37.770
they can move aside, and the molecules pile up in a so-called shockwave of energy. At precisely Mach 1,

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:42.880
if they were visible, spreading out in all directions, they might resemble a shallow dish

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:49.100
attached to the plane. But as the plane speed increases, the pressure wave sweeps back at an

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:56.520
angle, forming a funnel-shaped cone trailing back from the plane. And of course, a portion of this

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:58.920
funnel of pressure reaches the ground.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.700
When pressure of varying intensity and wavelength strikes the ear, we perceive it as sound.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:14.300
Therefore, anyone along the path of the cone of overpressure hears a sharp report similar to a thunderclap at the instant the cone passes.

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:16.590
There is the question of damage.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:27.570
In exhaustive tests conducted by structural engineers, even the flimsiest of buildings have remained unharmed through overpressures more than nine times greater than the probable boom.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:29.960
However, some windows...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.220
may be broken, particularly if the glass is already under stress. When the boom does strike

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:40.290
the ground, no one will try to tell you it's a pretty sound, but it is a comforting one.

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:47.280
It's the sound of your Air Force at work. Bill, this is Navigator. We're approaching

